		{
			dependencies: ["5C4E189A3E401566"]
			description: [
				"Here comes the chemistry."
				""
				"It might seem crazy right now, but you'll want a dedicated &3Chemical Reactor&r for a large fraction of the chemicals you want to process. You probably want to hold off on doing that until you've got a solid base with autocrafting though… it stings a lot less when you can just request crafts of Chemical Reactors from your AE2 system. Additionally, &3Large Chemical Reactors&r are easier to upgrade in the future."
			]
			id: "5357A16B5BF62C96"
			rewards: [{
				id: "184A514EEA6A4CCE"
				item: "kubejs:moni_nickel"
				type: "item"
			}]
			shape: "hexagon"
			tasks: [{
				id: "06864115DFFF1EDD"
				item: "gtceu:mv_chemical_reactor"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "MV Chemical Reactor"
			x: 13.5d
			y: 0.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: [
				"5357A16B5BF62C96"
				"5BE3E37772FAF6CB"
			]
			description: [
				"[\"\",{\"text\":\"This is an important part of a Processing Line. Visit the \"},{\"color\":\"yellow\",\"underlined\":true,\"text\":\"Renewable Production\",\"clickEvent\":{\"action\":\"change_page\",\"value\":\"055C0B43FE14258F\"}},{\"text\":\" tab to have more info, useful tips, and a visual representation of how to passively automate this.\"}]"
				""
				"&6Uraninite Ore&r is the first source of &6Pulsating Dust&r but is not a very good one in the long term. Instead you should make &6Resonant Clathrates&r, which are an infinitely-renewable and effective source of Pulsating Dust."
				""
				"Resonant Clathrates are made by melting &6Ender Pearls&r with a &3Fluid Extractor&r into &9Resonant Ender&r, then mixing that with &6Nether Quartz&r in an MV or better &3Chemical Reactor&r. Smelting this clathrate gives Pulsating Dust."
				""
				"With Resonant Clathrates now accessible, everything you need for crafting &6Pulsating Prediction Matrix&r can be automated in a net-positive loop.&r Doing so is the first step in automation of &bHostile Neural Networks&r, which is &ea primary source of important materials for the rest of the pack&r."
				""
				"The exact implementation is up to you. Make use of the various LV and MV machines you have, and look through &bEMI&r and the Renewable Production Tab for the ways to get infinite sources of each ingredient (like &aEnderman Models&r for pearls)."
			]
			disable_toast: true
			hide_until_deps_visible: true
			id: "0C10EC03A01E7334"
			optional: true
			shape: "hexagon"
			tasks: [{
				id: "5326C255DC39819A"
				item: "kubejs:resonant_clathrate"
				type: "item"
			}]
			x: 13.5d
			y: -1.75d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5357A16B5BF62C96"]
			description: [
				"Your &3Chemical Reactors&r will be making a lot of different fluids. You'll need to manage the logistics of routing and storing all the different fluids you're automating."
				""
				"Wherever possible, having one Chemical Reactor output directly into another machine that needs its output chemical saves you from having to figure out additional storage and routing. &aFluid Filters&r and &aPump covers&r are very useful for moving fluids around a compact processing pipeline."
				""
				"But what about the final output fluids that you need elsewhere?"
				"{@pagebreak}"
				"One option is to use the output tanks of the Chemical Reactors as a small storage buffer, letting it back up and stop crafting automatically. A &aStorage Bus&r on the machine will expose the fluid to your ME Network. Whenever you need some of this fluid, your AE2 system will pull the needed fluid out and the machine will resume processing."
				""
				"There's some catches with this approach though. First is that a &aStorage Bus&r is bidirectional by default. You will want to set the Storage Bus to &eExtract Only&r so AE doesn't try to store random fluids and items in the Chemical Reactor, and you may want to &ereduce the priority&r of the Storage Bus as well: this will signal to AE that you want it to remove fluids from that location first. Also, if multiple fluids are produced, &ethe machine will not resume processing if it doesn't have room for all fluid outputs&r."
				""
				"When you get access to them, it's probably just better to use single-fluid-formatted &aFluid Storage Cells&r and dump outputs into AE via Interfaces. You can fill up the cells, then pull the fluids out wherever else you need them. An alternative to this is storage buses on &3Gregtech Tanks and Drums&r."
			]
			icon: "gtceu:mv_electric_pump"
			id: "1D0DF2330B113B7A"
			shape: "hexagon"
			tasks: [{
				id: "516FB95F4092E0AB"
				type: "checkmark"
			}]
			title: "Fluid Logistics"
			x: 12.75d
			y: -0.75d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5C4E189A3E401566"]
			description: [
				"If you are distilling Ethanol, you now need an MV Distillery."
				""
				"&rThe &3Distillery&r is a single-block machine which only outputs one distillate out of several and &cdiscards&r the rest. You can use this now to get &9Ethanol&r from &9Biomass&r, or to selectively distill &9Oil&r in multiple steps to &9Ethylene&r. Its advantage over the full Tower, apart from cost, is its &9energy efficiency&r. Therefore, it is recommended for fluids with only one useful output, like &9Biomass&r, &9Diluted Sulfuric Acid&r, and &9Diluted Hydrochloric Acid&r."
				""
				"However, the Distillation Tower is recommended for advanced chemistry, as most things have more than one important output: &9Fermented Biomass&r, &9Charcoal Byproducts&r from your &3Pyrolyse Oven&r, and the entire line of petrochemicals from &9Oil&r, to name a few."
				""
				"You're going to need it soon!"
			]
			icon: "gtceu:mv_distillery"
			id: "48E83C38B37DFEE6"
			rewards: [{
				id: "14D06E944207ACDE"
				item: "kubejs:moni_nickel"
				type: "item"
			}]
			shape: "hexagon"
			tasks: [{
				id: "036F1F8553EEC984"
				item: {
					Count: 1
					id: "itemfilters:or"
					tag: {
						items: [
							{
								Count: 1b
								id: "gtceu:lv_distillery"
							}
							{
								Count: 1b
								id: "gtceu:mv_distillery"
							}
							{
								Count: 1b
								id: "gtceu:hv_distillery"
							}
						]
					}
				}
				title: "LV, MV, or HV Distillery"
				type: "item"
			}]
			title: "&2Distillery"
			x: 11.5d
			y: -2.75d
		}
		{
			dependencies: [
				"153BDCAE4D1CC407"
				"10722CBC4DAD28F4"
			]
			description: [
				"&9Vinyl Chloride&r is an industrial chemical made from chlorinating &9Ethylene&r (adding &9Chlorine&r at high temperature) in a &3Chemical Reactor&r. &9Ethane&r can be used as an alternative reagent."
				""
				"It is used to create the plastic &9Polyvinyl Chloride&r, most well known as PVC. While it deviates from reality, in GregTech you chemically react Vinyl Chloride and &9Oxygen&r to polymerize it into PVC. "
				""
				"Later, you can use &9Titanium Tetrachloride&r to catalyze this reaction as well for greater yield."
				""
				"You'll only need to solidify this into sheet form. If you want PVC pipes too, you can pulverise the sheet into dust and use that in the extruder, or you can solidify it into bars separately."
				""
				"&6PVC Sheets&r are superior to &6Polyethylene Sheets&r for making plastic substrates, producing twice as many &6Plastic Boards&r per craft."
			]
			id: "338ED53463203472"
			rewards: [{
				id: "2865986A098DD48B"
				item: "kubejs:moni_quarter"
				type: "item"
			}]
			shape: "hexagon"
			tasks: [{
